## Problem

In `src/command/autoremove.rs:75`:

```rust
let stdout = String::from_utf8_lossy(&query_output.stdout);
let packages: Vec<&str> = stdout.split_whitespace().collect();
```

`from_utf8_lossy` silently replaces any invalid UTF-8 bytes with `U+FFFD` (the replacement character). If `pacman -Qdtq` output contains invalid UTF-8, the mangled package names containing `\xef\xbf\xbd` are passed verbatim to `pacman -Rns --noconfirm`, which will:

1. Try to remove packages with corrupted names (pacman will reject them with an error), OR
2. In the unlikely scenario that a package name happens to still parse, remove a wrong package

The silent replacement violates the principle of failing loudly on unexpected input, especially when the downstream action is a destructive removal.

## Fix

Use `String::from_utf8` and return an error if the output is not valid UTF-8:

```rust
let stdout = match String::from_utf8(query_output.stdout) {
    Ok(s) => s,
    Err(_) => {
        eprintln!("yu: pacman produced non-UTF-8 output; aborting autoremove");
        return std::process::ExitCode::FAILURE;
    }
};
```

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `run_pacman_autoremove` fails with a clear error message when `pacman -Qdtq` produces non-UTF-8 output
- [ ] A unit test exercises the invalid-UTF-8 path via `pacman_query_orphans_cmd` output parsing logic
- [ ] Valid UTF-8 path continues to work as before
